-What will happen:
-• The normal kernel exploit and KernelSU late-load run first.
-• The app then starts the ReZygisk ptrace monitor and confirms that it attached to init.
-• After a short delay, a detached worker restarts zygote to trigger injection.
-• It verifies the KernelSU backend, ReZygisk daemon, zygote64 injection, and system_server.
-• If injection fails, the watchdog exits the monitor, disables ReZygisk, and restarts a clean zygote. A full watchdog reboot is still possible, which removes runtime root.
+Stage 1:
+• The app runs the normal exploit and late-loads KernelSU.
+• It does not touch the zygote or start the ReZygisk monitor yet.
+• When KernelSU is active, the app asks you to close it and open KernelSU.
+
+Stage 2:
+• Grant Root My Galaxy superuser access in KernelSU.
+• Return to Root My Galaxy. The home card will change to “KernelSU active — ReZygisk ready”.
+• Tap the card to start the monitor, schedule the controlled zygote restart, verify injection and system_server, or roll back on failure.
 
 Before starting:
 1. Install and enable ReZygisk in KernelSU.
 2. Remove or disable NeoZygisk, Zygisk Next, BreZygisk, and every other Zygisk provider.
 3. For the first test, disable Vector, TreatWheel, NoHello, and all other Zygisk modules.
-4. Do not run post-fs-data scripts manually and do not perform a soft reboot during the process.
-5. Keep the installer open until it reports completion or the controlled zygote restart closes it.
+4. Do not run post-fs-data scripts manually and do not perform a soft reboot yourself.
 
